8476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489139 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d469c932-3585-58b1-be22-0b73b73c3ef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1071 "The total area, in square meters,  covered by blueberry bushes in a forest can be modeled by the expression $800\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the year $2015.$ What does the base of the exponential expression $(1.1)$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "065bbf88-3b11-5079-b0c1-f463dc0506a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489175 #edb/ref 1074 "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=800$ represents the initial area covered by the  blueberry bushes\n\n- $1+r=1.1,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t$ is the number of years since $2015$\n\nSolving for $r$:\n\n$$\n1+r=1.1\\,⟹\\,r=0.1\n$$\n\nNow, $0.1$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $10%.$ Therefore, the area covered by the bushes grows at a rate of $10%$ per year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of an oil patch, measured in square kilometers, is given by the function $A(t)=10\\cdot (1.1)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in days since the pollution occurred. How many square kilometers does the area increase by between day $1$ and day $2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38bb781d-e24d-5ca2-821e-7901089bd6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489204 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the growth of the patch between day $1$ and day $2$, we need to subtract the area after day $1$ from the area after day $2.$ So we need to calculate\n\n$$\nA(2)-A(1).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the area after the first day, $A(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(1) & =10\\cdot (1.1)^{1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1.1 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $A(2),$ the area after the second day:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2) & =10\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1.21 \\\\  & =12.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth of the patch between day $1$ and day $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2)-A(1) & =12.1-11=1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area increased by $1.1$ square kilometer between day $1$ and day $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1071 "The population $M(t)$ of a colony of rodents is given by $M(t)=1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in years since the beginning of the observations. How many additional rodents will be born in the colony between years $2$ and $3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "978bca7b-e158-5bd2-ae01-fa65d0556739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489246 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the growth in the population of rodents between year $2$ and year $3$, we need to subtract the population after year $2$ from the population after year $3.$ So we need to calculate\n\n$$\nM(3)-M(2).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the population after the second year, $M(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(2) & =1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{(2)} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\cdot 2.56 \\\\  & =2\\,560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $M(3),$ the population after the third year:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(3) & =1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{(3)} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\cdot 4.096 \\\\  & =4\\,096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the population between year $2$ and year $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(3)-M(2) & =4\\,096-2\\,560=1\\,536.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the population of rodents increased by $1\\,536$ between years $2$ and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489341 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489341 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92b6b921-bfab-5106-aebc-d9c7e4e38733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489341 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2e8828f-e8f0-5f6b-8f2a-4544652c7ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489361 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c81e5c67-ac42-5acf-b9ba-dd9c76587fb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489361 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489204 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
